contentText:
Megan Thee Stallion joined Michelle Obama&#8217;s IMO podcast on Wednesday (Aug. 12), during which she shed light on her feelings that the internet has allowed artists with &#8220;terrible&#8221; music to achieve viral fame too easily.

Obama&#8217;s cohost Craig Robinson learned that Megan&#8217;s mother was an artist who taught her how to be a professional and go about her business in music, while others in this current landscape &#8220;want the fame without the hard work that goes into it.&#8221;

“I mean, honestly, it&#8217;s a little bit easier to do that, though, now,&#8221; Megan agreed. &#8220;You can go viral on Instagram and have a terrible song.&#8221;

She continued: &#8220;The internet has made it really easy to be famous &#8212; good or bad. I think you can tell the difference between people who really love what they do and people who just want to be famous. I feel like I fall on the side of I really love what I do.”

Robinson thinks that artists who don&#8217;t truly love their craft last, but Megan said even those who don&#8217;t can still &#8220;sometimes&#8221; carve out a lengthy career.

While the Houston Hottie has continued to tease her upcoming Act: III album, the &#8220;WAP&#8221; rapper confirmed the project will welcome the return of her Tina Snow alter ego.

&#8220;A reintroduction for Hotties that may have not been with me since 2017, 2018; I think this will be a new experience for them,&#8221; she said. &#8220;I think I&#8217;m just reintroducing my alter ego, Tina Snow. Tina is grown now. She has been through lots of life since you heard from her last.

&#8220;I have so many collaborations that I feel like the Hotties have been asking for, from niche collaborations, collaborations that&#8217;s overdue, collaborations that shock the people,&#8221; Meg added. &#8220;I feel like Act: III is gonna be one of my favorite projects that I ever put out.&#8221;

The 31-year-old&#8217;s most album, Megan , came in June 2024. It  debuted at No. 3  on the  Billboard 200  with 64,000 total album-equivalent units, according to Luminate. She returned with the  Act: II  deluxe for the LP in October of the same year.

